// A text table: cells of any type that renders, laid out in columns that line up.
//
// The axis this owns is **text measured for display**. Every other program in the set treats a
// string as bytes to be copied or compared; this one has to know how wide one *looks*, because a
// column is a promise about where the next border falls. Nothing else in the set asks.
//
// Deliberately not modelled: multiple border styles, Markdown and TSV output, row spanning, and
// wrapping a cell that is wider than its column. Each is more of the same layout; none of them asks
// the language anything the box below does not.
//
//
// What writing it found
// ---------------------
//
// **A `FormatSpec`'s width is a byte count, so it cannot lay out a column.** `14 §2` says width and
// precision count bytes "as C's do", and `display_pad` pads to that. A table is the program that
// minds: `café` is five bytes and four columns, so a field padded to a byte count is short by one —
// and it is short by **one per non-ASCII character**, so two cells of one column are wrong by
// different amounts and the column comes out ragged rather than merely narrow. There is no
// correction to apply afterwards.
//
// So this program ignores the specifier's width entirely, hands every cell the neutral spec, and
// does its own padding over a count it asks `sysl.text` for (`columns`). That is the right division
// — only the caller knows what a column is — but it means the one field in `FormatSpec` a table
// would have used is the one it cannot use, and a program that trusted it would produce a table
// that is wrong only for text that is not ASCII, which is the worst way to be wrong.
//
// Decided in `14 §2`: the specifier keeps counting bytes and keeps its equivalence with `snprintf`,
// because a specifier is for `printf`-shaped output; laying anything out asks `columns` instead.
//
// **A correction rather than a finding, and it is the same one `png` recorded: ask the library
// before reporting an absence.** This program was most of the way to writing up "a rendered value
// cannot become a `string`" — `ByteSink.text()` answers a `[]u8`, and two earlier programs had
// wanted `from_utf8` and not had it. It is there now, in `sysl.text`, and it takes the rendered
// bytes exactly as the chapter says. What that would have cost was not just a false note: the
// checks were written twice, once comparing bytes by hand and printing them back through
// `putbytes`, before the conversion was looked for. `04` had specified it all along.
//
// **A character is not a column either — found here, and now fixed in the library.** The first
// version of this program counted characters, which is right for `café` and `Zürich` and wrong for
// `日本`: two characters occupying four terminal columns. It was recorded as a boundary rather than
// solved, on the grounds that the East Asian Width table from UAX #11 is data rather than language
// and had nowhere to live.
//
// **That reason was already false when it was written**, which is the more useful half of the
// finding. Module-level `const` and `val` had landed the day before (`13 §7`), and four other
// programs in this set were already using them — `guide/datetime` carries a transition table built
// by a function at module level, which is the exact shape a width table needs. An absence is worth
// checking against the language as it is *today* rather than as it was when the habit formed; this
// one cost a paragraph explaining why something could not be done that could.
//
// So `columns` is now `sysl.text`'s, backed by the Unicode Character Database, and this program
// measures `日本` and a decomposed `café` as correctly as it measures ASCII. What the program keeps
// is the *call*, not the table: only the caller knows where the next border falls.
//
// **A `require` cannot carry its message onto the next line.** Continuation covers an operator that
// cannot finish an expression, and brackets already join — but `require cond, "message"` has no
// brackets, so its comma cannot carry the line and a long message has nowhere to go. Every other
// multi-line call in this directory continues inside brackets and is fine.
//
// Decided and left as it is (`00 §9`), and the reason is the rule rather than the effort: a comma
// **can** finish an expression, since the message is optional, so continuing there would need the
// rule to consult which construct it is inside — which is what the whole design avoids. What would
// fix it without touching the rule is bracketing the operands, and that is a change to how a
// contract is spelled.
//
// **The payoff, and it is the whole reason this program is writable at all: a heterogeneous row is
// ordinary code.** `t.add(["totals", 7, 340282366920938463463374607431768211455u128, true])` is an
// array literal of four unrelated types coercing to `[]const &Display`, each boxed and erased at
// the element. Until every built-in reached `Display` through an `impl` (`14 §5`), an integer could
// not be erased at all — so a table of numbers, which is what a table is usually for, could not be
// spelled. This program was the thing that asked for that, and it is now the thing that shows it.
//
// A smaller positive beside it: a fixed array does **not** decay to a slice at a call, so a row
// built as a binding is passed `row[..]` — but an array literal written *at* the argument coerces
// directly, which is why the rows above read the way they do.

import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf, byte_sink}
import sysl.text.columns

// How a cell's text sits in the field its column gives it.
enum Align
    Left
    Right
    Center
end Align

// One rendered cell: what the value came to, and how wide that is on screen.
//
// Both are kept because both are wanted and the rendering is what produces them. A table cannot lay
// out a column until it knows the widest cell in it, so it has to see every cell before it writes
// the first -- and rendering each value a second time at that point would mean holding the values
// instead, which is the same storage with a dispatch on the end of it.
struct Cell
    text: &Buf[u8]
    width: usize
end Cell

// Render a value once, keeping the bytes and measuring them.
//
// The parameter is a `&Display` rather than a `T: Display`, and that is what lets a row hold a
// `string` beside an `int`: a bound is a promise about **one** type and a row is a sequence of
// different ones. Every built-in reaches the trait through an `impl`, so an integer erases here
// exactly as a program's own struct does (`14 §5`).
cell_of(v: &Display) -> Cell
    var sink = byte_sink()
    var out: *Writer = &sink

    v.display(out, FormatSpec(0, -1, false))

    Cell(sink.bytes, columns(sink.text()))
end cell_of

// A table of rendered cells, in row-major order.
//
// The cells are **one flat buffer** rather than a buffer of rows: a row is `ncols` consecutive
// entries, so a column is a walk with a stride and neither shape needs a nested container. The first
// row is the header.
struct Table
    ncols: usize
    aligns: &Buf[Align]
    cells: &Buf[Cell]

    rows(self) -> usize = self.cells.len() / self.ncols

    // Add a row. The cells are rendered here rather than at layout, so a value handed to a table
    // needs to outlive the call and nothing longer.
    add(*self, values: []const &Display)
        require values.len == self.ncols, "a row has to have one cell per column"

        for v in values do self.cells.push(cell_of(v))
    end add

    // The width of a column: its widest cell, the header included.
    width(self, col: usize) -> usize
        var w = 0usize
        var i = col

        while i < self.cells.len()
            val c = self.cells.at(i)

            if c.width > w then w = c.width

            i += self.ncols

        w
    end width

    // `+---+---+`, which is what makes a column that is laid out wrongly visible rather than merely
    // wrong.
    rule(self, out: *Writer)
        var c = 0usize

        while c < self.ncols
            out.write("+".bytes)
            fill(out, 45u8, self.width(c) + 2usize)
            c += 1usize

        out.write("+\n".bytes)
    end rule

    line(self, r: usize, out: *Writer)
        var c = 0usize

        while c < self.ncols
            val cell = self.cells.at(r * self.ncols + c)
            val slack = self.width(c) - cell.width
            val left = self.aligns.at(c) match
                Left   -> 0usize
                Right  -> slack
                Center -> slack / 2usize

            out.write("| ".bytes)
            fill(out, 32u8, left)
            out.write(cell.text.view())
            fill(out, 32u8, slack - left)
            out.write(" ".bytes)

            c += 1usize

        out.write("|\n".bytes)
    end line

    render(self, out: *Writer)
        var r = 0usize

        self.rule(out)

        while r < self.rows()
            self.line(r, out)

            // Under the header, and nowhere else: one rule between the names and the data is what
            // says which row the names are.
            if r == 0usize then self.rule(out)

            r += 1usize

        self.rule(out)
    end render
end Table

table(aligns: []const Align) -> Table
    var a: &Buf[Align] = buf()

    a.extend(aligns)

    Table(aligns.len, a, buf())
end table

// One byte, `n` times.
fill(out: *Writer, b: u8, n: usize)
    var i = 0usize

    while i < n
        out.write([b][..])
        i += 1usize
end fill
